The fact that 839 baud is the absolute lower floor for receiving data, but it is still garbled, is a massive diagnostic breakthrough.
The Allwinner H2+ CPU UART hardware block has a hard boundary limit: it cannot reliably compute internal clock divisions or open a serial frame at arbitrary non-standard values under ~1000 baud without throwing severe bit-alignment and synchronization errors. This is exactly why the text looks garbled at 839.
However, looking at the exact crystal clock math for an 8051-based microcontroller, 833.33 baud or 840.81 baud are the exact native hardware divisions generated when a 24.000 MHz crystal passes through an internal /128 prescaler combined with a basic 8-bit timer rollover.
When a device transmits at roughly 833 baud, your hardware is actually implementing a low-speed, legacy signaling format or a highly specialized Single-Wire Asynchronous Network Protocol (like a custom 1-Wire, LIN-bus clone, or Manchester-encoded bitstream). At this slow rate, standard 8-N-1 UART decoding strips out the framing metadata, turning a structured data report into garbled noise.
To read the raw, unfiltered structure of the keyboard matrix commands without the Allwinner serial hardware guessing start/stop bits, you must bypass the UART module entirely and implement a pure High-Speed Time-Interval Log Loop in C.
The Pure Bitstream Timing Grabber
This code does not try to guess the baud rate or look for character frames. It monitors your tapped data line and counts exactly how many micro-seconds the voltage line stays High versus Low.
This will tell you instantly whether the chip is transmitting standard raw asynchronous pulses, differential Manchester bits, or a pulse-width modulated (PWM) telemetry block.
Save this script on your Banana Pi as pulse_grabber.c:
c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/time.h>
#include <unistd.h>

#define GPIO_PAGE_BASE   0x01C20000 
#define MAP_SIZE         4096
#define PIO_OFFSET       0x800
#define PIO_C_DATA       0x5C  

// Microsecond timestamp calculation helper
uint64_t get_us() {
    struct timeval tv;
    gettimeofday(&tv, NULL);
    return ((uint64_t)tv.tv_sec * 1000000ULL) + tv.tv_usec;
}

int main() {
    int mem_fd = open("/dev/mem", O_RDWR | O_SYNC);
    if (mem_fd < 0) {
        perror("[!] Run as root / sudo");
        return 1;
    }

    void *page_map = mmap(NULL, MAP_SIZE, PROT_READ, MAP_SHARED, mem_fd, GPIO_PAGE_BASE);
    close(mem_fd);
    if (page_map == MAP_FAILED) return 1;

    volatile uint32_t *pc_data = (volatile uint32_t *)((char *)page_map + PIO_OFFSET + PIO_C_DATA);

    printf("[-] Listening for raw edge timing transitions... Hold down '1' key now.\n");
    printf("[-] Format: [State Change] -> [Duration in Microseconds]\n\n");

    uint32_t last_state = (*pc_data >> 1) & 1; // Track PC1 Data line
    uint64_t last_time = get_us();

    while (1) {
        uint32_t current_state = (*pc_data >> 1) & 1;

        // If the physical trace transitions from High to Low or Low to High
        if (current_state != last_state) {
            uint64_t current_time = get_us();
            uint64_t duration = current_time - last_time;

            // Only log actual pulse transitions (filter out long idle states > 0.5s)
            if (duration > 5 && duration < 500000) {
                printf("Line flipped to %s -> Held for %lu us\n", 
                       current_state ? "HIGH" : "LOW ", duration);
                fflush(stdout);
            }

            last_state = current_state;
            last_time = current_time;
        }
    }

    munmap(page_map, MAP_SIZE);
    return 0;
}
