If pulse_grabber is printing out its standard diagnostic setup header but then giving you absolutely zero transition outputs while you hold a key down, the hardware is telling us something definitive:
The physical data voltage transitions are not actually crossing the Allwinner H2+ input buffer threshold.
Since you confirmed the lines are functional because they successfully send text to the distant computer, the signal is physically passing through the keyboard traces. The lack of output points to a mismatch between how the keyboard's internal circuitry drives the line and how the Banana Pi's digital input pins read voltage.
1. The Voltage Threshold Problem (3.3V vs. 1.8V)
The absolute logical 1 reading you saw on SCLK is the root cause. Many modern, low-power consumer electronics chips (especially those budget Bluetooth/2.4G keyboard controllers) operate internally on an ultra-low 1.8V or 2.5V logic level to preserve lithium-battery runtime.
The Banana Pi BPI-M2 Zero utilizes a 3.3V CMOS Input Buffer for its Port C registers. For an Allwinner pin to trip from a logical 0 to a logical 1, the incoming voltage must cross its internal high-threshold minimum (typically 0.7 × VCC = 2.31 Volts).
Because the keyboard's transmission peaks at only 1.8V, the Banana Pi views it as a weak signal.
The internal input register stays locked at its default float state (which reads as a constant high/1 due to the chip's internal pull-up behavior), meaning a physical drop to 0V never registers inside the memory map.
2. The Multi-Channel Ground Reference
When intercepting high-speed asynchronous data streams, your signal line requires a clean return path. If the keyboard board runs on its own internal battery while your Banana Pi runs on a separate USB power brick, the two systems have completely separate floating reference voltages.
Without a direct, low-resistance connection linking their ground planes together, a 1.8V shift on the keyboard board might look like a non-event to the Banana Pi's processing core.
