Moving your tap wires completely away from the main 40-pin header and connecting them directly to the dedicated 3-pin UART console header (the small unpopulated pads sitting alone near the processor or edges of the board) changes the configuration significantly.
On the Banana Pi BPI-M2 Zero (and almost all Allwinner H2+/H3 boards), that dedicated 3-pin serial port is hardwired to UART0 [index:1.3.5].
Because UART0 acts as the low-level operating system architecture's native Linux System Debug Console, trying to listen to standard peripheral devices on those lines will cause two major hardware issues:
1. The Linux Kernel Console Lock
Because UART0 is the default system debug console, the Linux kernel has an absolute, high-priority ownership lock on it.
The second the operating system boots up, it assigns a background program called agetty or serial-getty to listen to the RX line on that header.
When your keyboard chip fires asynchronous byte streams into that RX via, the Linux kernel intercepts them, assumes a user is typing terminal commands, and tries to respond by spawning a bash shell prompt. Your python scripts or picocom commands will read absolute silence because the operating system is stripping the incoming data bytes away at a lower driver level.
2. The 1500000 Baud Mismatch Block
The Linux bootloader (U-Boot) and kernel on Allwinner processors drive UART0 at an extremely high default speed: 115200 or a specialized high-tier 1500000 (1.5 Mbps) baud rate [index:1.3.3]. If your keyboard matrix chip is trickling down data at a different transmission speed, the data frames will misalign with the system console timing loop.
🛠️ How to Safely Tap and Read via the Dedicated Header
To use this header as a clean peripheral input port without background noise or kernel lockouts, you must configure the operating system to release the port:
Step 1: Tell Linux to Stop Listening to the Port
You need to disable the system console manager so the kernel stops stealing incoming bytes from the RX pin. Run these commands from your terminal prompt:
bash
# Force the system manager to stop the serial login terminal tracking loop
sudo systemctl stop serial-getty@ttyS0.service
sudo systemctl disable serial-getty@ttyS0.service
Use code with caution.
Step 2: Clear the Boot Arguments
The kernel is instructed to send text boot logs out of this port via the primary boot arguments file.
Open the system environment config:
bash
sudo nano /boot/armbianEnv.txt
Use code with caution.
Look for any variables containing console=ttyS0,115200 or console=display.
If your image handles arguments inside an independent boot script file, open that configuration path as well:
bash
