1-Wire and UART look superficially identical when probed with a basic multimeter because they both use a single, active-low data wire that sits at a logical High (1) when idling [index:1.3.4, index:1.3.5]. However, underneath that electrical similarity, their internal timing frameworks, line synchronization mechanisms, and data encoding rules are completely incompatible [index:1.3.5, index:1.3.6]. 
1. 1-Wire vs. UART: The Core Technical Difference
The critical architectural difference lies in how the two protocols define a binary 1 and 0 in time.
Standard UART (Asynchronous Framing)
UART is purely based on a fixed time duration (Baud Rate) [index:1.3.5]. The receiving system starts a blind timer the absolute microsecond the line drops low (the Start Bit) [index:1.1.2, index:1.1.4]. It then samples the center of each bit window sequentially [index:1.1.2, index:1.1.3]. If the line is low during that window, it's a 0; if it's high, it's a 1 [index:1.1.2]. 
Standard 1-Wire (Dallas/Maxim Protocol) 
1-Wire does not care about fixed bit windows [index:1.3.7]. It uses Pulse-Width Time-Slots managed by an open-drain bus master [index:1.3.4]. Every single bit transmitted—whether a 1 or a 0—begins with the exact same action: the transmitter pulls the line Low to wake up the receiver [index:1.3.1]. 
To send a 0: The transmitter holds the line Low for a long time (~60 microseconds) [index:1.3.4].
To send a 1: The transmitter pulls the line Low briefly (~1 to 15 microseconds) and releases it to snap back High [index:1.3.4]. 
UART '1'   _______________________ (Stays HIGH)
UART '0'   \______________________ (Stays LOW)

1-Wire '1' \___/~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ (Pulsed LOW briefly, spikes back HIGH)
1-Wire '0' \____________________/  (Held LOW for nearly the whole slot)
Why it's "Un-decryptable" to the Linux Driver
If you pipe a 1-Wire signal into a UART receiver (or vice versa), the UART engine sees every single 1-Wire bit transition as a UART Start Bit [index:1.3.1]. The UART hardware block attempts to open a brand-new 8-bit character structure on every single pulse, causing constant framing alignment crashes, parity validation failures, and the randomized string of garbage symbols you're observing [index:1.1.7].
2. What Else Could the Protocol Be?
Given that you are dealing with a custom multi-mode keyboard/touchpad SoC, if standard UART and the basic Linux 1-Wire overlay are returning garbage data, the chip is highly likely utilizing one of three ultra-budget single-wire consumer electronic interfaces:
Candidate A: Manchester Encoding
Used extensively by budget wireless chipsets to pass data cleanly through cheap single-layer PCBs without requiring tight clock synchronization. Instead of mapping high/low voltages to bits, the data is embedded directly in the direction of the transition itself:
A transition from Low to High in the center of a bit window defines a logical 1.
A transition from High to Low in the center of a bit window defines a logical 0.
Because the voltage line flips up and down constantly mid-frame to maintain synchronization, a standard UART hardware receiver interprets this as a rapid sequence of arbitrary start and stop framing breaks.
Candidate B: Custom PWM / Strobe Telemetry
Many low-cost touchpads do not speak standard digital protocols. They run a basic internal RC timer that stretches a single continuous Pulse-Width Modulated (PWM) pulse width relative to the coordinate inputs. For example, moving a finger along the 
-axis alters the active low duration from 100 microseconds up to 800 microseconds.
Candidate C: 1-Wire Bus Emulation Mode
Some modern microcontrollers feature a dedicated UART-to-1-Wire Emulation mode [index:1.3.6]. They artificially run the hardware UART serial engine at 115,200 baud but modify the data bytes sent to simulate a 1-Wire pulse wave [index:1.3.1]. To send a single 1-Wire bit, the controller transmits an entire 8-bit UART byte (like 0x00 or 0xFF), using the serial stop bits to shape the waveform [index:1.3.1, index:1.3.4]. 
